Lamlet standard library (std), language version 0.1.

This file is an ordinary Lamlet document: only dollar regions are active, and this prose is a comment. Every
definition is ordinary Lamlet over the primitives of the built-in package K (docs/spec.md, section 8). A name
starting with `_` is private. Each definition's doc is the paragraph directly above it.

1. Functions

Fn([A], A). Returns its argument unchanged.
$[# {id x} x]

Fn([A, B], A). Returns x; the second argument is still evaluated.
$[# {const x ignored} x]

Fn([Fn([B], C), Fn([A], B)], Fn([A], C)). {compose f g} is the function x -> {f {g x}}.
$[# {compose f g} {^ {x} {f {g x}}}]

Fn([Fn([A, B], C)], Fn([B, A], C)). The same function with its two arguments swapped.
$[# {flip f} {^ {x y} {f y x}}]

Fn([String], A). Aborts the whole evaluation with the message; it never returns, so it fits any type.
$[# {error message} {K::fail message}]

Unit. The only value of type Unit.
$[# unit K::unit]

Fn([A], String). A deterministic display of any value, for diagnostics (not for presentation).
$[# debug K::debugString]

2. Booleans and control

Fn([Bool, Fn([], A), Fn([], A)], A). Calls the first thunk if the condition is true, otherwise the second.
The first-class form of !if.
$[# caseBool K::caseBool]

{!if c then else}: evaluates the Bool c, then only the selected branch. Both branches are checked and must
have the same type.
$[Macro !if
    {{c then else} {K::caseBool c {^ {} then} {^ {} else}}}]

{!and e...}: true when every operand is true; evaluates left to right and stops at the first false.
$[Macro !and
    {{} true}
    {{first rest ...} {!if first {!and rest ...} false}}]

{!or e...}: true when some operand is true; evaluates left to right and stops at the first true.
$[Macro !or
    {{} false}
    {{first rest ...} {!if first true {!or rest ...}}}]

Fn([Bool], Bool). Logical negation.
$[# {not b} {!if b false true}]

Fn([Bool, A, A], A). Selects x if b is true, otherwise y. Both are already evaluated: use !if to avoid that.
$[# {choose b x y} {!if b x y}]

3. Equality and order

Eq A: Fn([A, A], Bool). Structural equality; functions and native values have none (a type error).
$[# = K::equal]

Eq A: Fn([A, A], Bool). True when the values differ.
$[# {!= a b} {not {= a b}}]

Ord A: Fn([A, A], Bool). Strictly less. Int, Float, and String (by Unicode scalar) are ordered.
$[# < K::less]

Ord A: Fn([A, A], Bool). Strictly greater.
$[# {> a b} {< b a}]

Ord A: Fn([A, A], Bool). Less or equal.
$[# {<= a b} {not {< b a}}]

Ord A: Fn([A, A], Bool). Greater or equal.
$[# {>= a b} {not {< a b}}]

Ord A: Fn([A, A], A). The smaller value (the first, on ties).
$[# {min a b} {!if {< b a} b a}]

Ord A: Fn([A, A], A). The larger value (the first, on ties).
$[# {max a b} {!if {< a b} b a}]

Ord A: Fn([A, A, A], A). x limited to the range from lo to hi.
$[# {clamp x lo hi} {min {max x lo} hi}]

4. Numbers

Num N: Fn([N, N], N). Addition (Int overflow is an error; no mixing of Int and Float).
$[# + K::add]

Num N: Fn([N, N], N). Subtraction.
$[# - K::subtract]

Num N: Fn([N, N], N). Multiplication.
$[# * K::multiply]

Fn([Float, Float], Float). Division; dividing by zero is an error. Use div for Int.
$[# / K::divideFloat]

Num N: Fn([N], N). Negation.
$[# neg K::negate]

Num N: Fn([N], N). The magnitude; abs of the minimum Int overflows.
$[# {abs x} {!if {< x {- x x}} {neg x} x}]

Fn([Int, Int], Int). Euclidean quotient: a = b*q + r with 0 <= r < abs(b).
$[# div K::quotientEuclidean]

Fn([Int, Int], Int). Euclidean remainder, always in [0, abs(b)): {% -8 3} is 1.
$[# % K::remainderEuclidean]

Fn([Int], Float). Converts, rounding large integers to the nearest Float.
$[# float K::toFloat]

Fn([Float], Int). The largest Int not above x.
$[# floor K::floor]

Fn([Float], Int). The smallest Int not below x.
$[# ceil K::ceil]

Fn([Float], Int). The nearest Int, halves away from zero ({round 2.5} is 3).
$[# round K::round]

Fn([Float], Int). Drops the fraction, rounding toward zero.
$[# truncate K::truncate]

Fn([Float, Int], String). x with exactly n decimal places (0 to 20), correctly rounded, ties to even.
$[# fixed K::fixed]

Fn([Float], Float). Square root; negative arguments are an error.
$[# sqrt K::sqrt]

Fn([Float], Float). Sine (radians), computed identically on every platform.
$[# sin K::sin]

Fn([Float], Float). Cosine (radians), computed identically on every platform.
$[# cos K::cos]

Fn([Float], Float). e to the power x.
$[# exp K::exp]

Fn([Float], Float). Natural logarithm; the argument must be positive.
$[# ln K::ln]

Float. The ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter.
$[# pi 3.141592653589793]

Fn([Float, Float], Float). x to the power y, for x >= 0 (0 to any power is 0).
$[# {pow x y} {!if {= x 0.0} 0.0 {exp {* y {ln x}}}}]

Fn([Float, Float, Float], Float). Linear interpolation: a at t = 0, b at t = 1.
$[# {lerp a b t} {+ a {* {- b a} t}}]

5. Lists

Fn([A, List(A)], List(A)). Prepends one element.
$[# cons K::cons]

Fn([List(A), Fn([], R), Fn([A, List(A)], R)], R). Calls the first handler for the empty list, or the second
with the head and tail.
$[# caseList K::caseList]

Fn([List(A), B, Fn([B, A], B)], B). Left fold: combines the accumulator with each element, first to last.
$[# {fold xs acc f}
    {K::caseList xs {^ {} acc} {^ {x rest} {fold rest {f acc x} f}}}
]

Fn([List(A)], List(A)). The elements in reverse order.
$[# {reverse xs} {fold xs {'} {^ {acc x} {cons x acc}}}]

Fn([List(A), B, Fn([A, B], B)], B). Strict right fold: callbacks run from the last element to the first.
$[# {foldr xs acc f} {fold {reverse xs} acc {^ {a x} {f x a}}}]

Fn([List(A), Fn([A], B)], List(B)). Applies f to each element, in order.
$[# {map xs f} {reverse {fold xs {'} {^ {acc x} {cons {f x} acc}}}}]

Fn([List(A), Fn([A], Bool)], List(A)). The elements for which p is true, in order.
$[# {filter xs p} {reverse {fold xs {'} {^ {acc x} {!if {p x} {cons x acc} acc}}}}]

Fn([List(A)], Bool). True for the empty list.
$[# {isEmpty xs} {K::caseList xs {^ {} true} {^ {x rest} false}}]

Fn([List(A)], A). The first element; errors on an empty list.
$[# {head xs} {K::caseList xs {^ {} {error "head of an empty list"}} {^ {x rest} x}}]

Fn([List(A)], List(A)). Everything after the first element; errors on an empty list.
$[# {tail xs} {K::caseList xs {^ {} {error "tail of an empty list"}} {^ {x rest} rest}}]

Fn([List(A)], Int). The number of elements.
$[# {length xs} {fold xs 0 {^ {n x} {+ n 1}}}]

Fn([List(A), List(A)], List(A)). xs followed by ys.
$[# {append xs ys} {foldr xs ys cons}]

Fn([List(List(A))], List(A)). Concatenates a list of lists, one level deep.
$[# {flatten xss} {foldr xss {'} append}]

Fn([List(A), Int], A). The element at a zero-based index; errors when it is out of range.
$[# {nth xs i}
    {K::caseList xs {^ {} {error "nth: index out of range"}} {^ {x rest} {!if {= i 0} x {nth rest {- i 1}}}}}
]

Fn([Int, Int], List(Int)). The Ints from `from` up to, but not including, `to`.
$[# {range from to}
    [# {go i acc} {!if {< i from} acc {go {- i 1} {cons i acc}}}]
    {go {- to 1} {'}}
]

Fn([List(A), Int], List(A)). At most the first n elements.
$[# {take xs n}
    [# {go ys k acc} {!if {<= k 0} acc {K::caseList ys {^ {} acc} {^ {y rest} {go rest {- k 1} {cons y acc}}}}}]
    {reverse {go xs n {'}}}
]

Fn([List(A), Int], List(A)). Everything after the first n elements.
$[# {drop xs n}
    {!if {<= n 0} xs {K::caseList xs {^ {} {'}} {^ {x rest} {drop rest {- n 1}}}}}
]

Fn([List(A), Fn([A], Bool)], Bool). True when p holds for some element (stops at the first).
$[# {any xs p}
    {K::caseList xs {^ {} false} {^ {x rest} {!or {p x} {any rest p}}}}
]

Fn([List(A), Fn([A], Bool)], Bool). True when p holds for every element (stops at the first failure).
$[# {all xs p}
    {K::caseList xs {^ {} true} {^ {x rest} {!and {p x} {all rest p}}}}
]

Fn([List(A), List(B)], List({first: A, second: B})). Pairs elements up to the shorter list's length.
$[# {zip xs ys}
    {K::caseList xs {^ {} {'}} {^ {x xr}
        {K::caseList ys {^ {} {'}} {^ {y yr} {cons {. first x second y} {zip xr yr}}}}
    }}
]

Eq A: Fn([List(A), A], Bool). True when some element equals the value.
$[# {contains xs value} {any xs {^ {x} {= x value}}}]

Eq A: Fn([List(A)], List(A)). Keeps the first occurrence of each value, in order.
$[# {unique xs}
    {reverse {fold xs {'} {^ {seen x} {!if {contains seen x} seen {cons x seen}}}}}
]

Eq A: Fn([List(A), List(A)], List(A)). Concatenates two lists and keeps the first occurrence of each value.
$[# {union xs ys} {unique {append xs ys}}]

6. Records, variants, options, and pairs

Records are written {. label value ...}; .label reads a field and .label= replaces one. Variants are built
with #label and taken apart with match and a record of handlers, one per alternative.

Fn([V, H], R). {match v {. a fa b fb}} calls the handler of v's alternative with its payload. The handlers
must cover exactly the variant's alternatives.
$[# match K::match]

{!case v {alt x body} ...}: matches v, binding the payload of each alternative to x in its body. It is
{match v {. alt {^ {x} body} ...}}, so the clauses must cover exactly the variant's alternatives. A Unit
payload still has a name: {!case o {none u 0} {some x x}}.
$[Macro !case
    {{v {alt x body} ...} {match v {. {... alt {^ {x} body}} ...}}}]

Fn([A, B], {first: A, second: B}). A pair is an ordinary record.
$[# {pair a b} {. first a second b}]

Fn([{first: A, ...}], A). The first component of a pair (or any record with a `first` field).
$[# first .first]

Fn([{second: B, ...}], B). The second component of a pair.
$[# second .second]

Fn([A], <none: Unit, some: A>). A present optional value.
$[# some #some]

<none: Unit, some: A>. The absent optional value.
$[# none [% {#none K::unit}]]

Fn([<none: Unit, some: A>, Fn([], R), Fn([A], R)], R). Calls ifNone for none, or ifSome with the payload.
$[# {caseOption o ifNone ifSome} {match o {. none {^ {u} {ifNone}} some ifSome}}]

Fn([<none: Unit, some: A>, A], A). The payload, or the default for none.
$[# {withDefault o default} {caseOption o {^ {} default} id}]

7. Types

Type values are ordinary data describing types. They are used by ascription {: e T}, whose type is computed at
compile time, and by native declarations, where they must be statically known.

The Type of signed 64-bit integers.
$[# Int K::intType]

The Type of finite binary64 numbers.
$[# Float K::floatType]

The Type of true and false.
$[# Bool K::boolType]

The Type of Unicode scalar sequences.
$[# String K::stringType]

The Type whose only value is unit.
$[# Unit K::unitType]

The Type of Type values.
$[# Type K::typeType]

The Type of type-erased values (toData): data of any comparable type, kept for the host to read. A Data value
cannot be inspected in Lamlet; it can only be compared with =.
$[# Data K::dataType]

Eq A: Fn([A], Data). Forgets the value's type, keeping the value, so values of different types can share
one List or field. Functions and native values are rejected by the check.
$[# toData K::toData]

Fn([Type], Type). The Type of lists of T.
$[# List K::listType]

Fn([List(Type), Type], Type). {Fn {' A B} R} is the Type of functions from A and B to R.
$[# Fn K::functionType]

Fn([String, Type], {name: String, type: Type}). A named member, for Struct and Variant.
$[# {Field name T} {. name name type T}]

Fn([List({name: String, type: Type})], Type). A structural record Type; member order does not matter.
$[# Struct K::recordType]

Fn([List({name: String, type: Type})], Type). A structural variant Type; member order does not matter.
$[# Variant K::variantType]

The empty variant: the Type of an expression that never returns.
$[# Never [% {Variant {'}}]]

Fn([Type], Type). The Type of optional values of T.
$[# {Option T} {Variant {' {Field "none" Unit} {Field "some" T}}}]

Fn([Type, Type], Type). The Type of pairs.
$[# {Pair A B} {Struct {' {Field "first" A} {Field "second" B}}}]

8. Strings

Scalar A: Fn([A], String). Formats a String, Int, Float, Bool, or Unit (Unit gives the empty String).
$[# str K::scalarString]

Fn([String, String], String). Concatenates two Strings.
$[# ++ K::stringAppend]

Fn([String], List(String)). The String's Unicode scalars, each as a one-scalar String.
$[# chars K::stringScalars]

Fn([List(String)], String). Concatenates a list of Strings.
$[# {concat xs} {fold xs "" ++}]

Fn([String, List(String)], String). Concatenates with sep between neighbours.
$[# {join sep xs}
    {K::caseList xs {^ {} ""} {^ {x rest} {fold rest x {^ {acc y} {++ acc {++ sep y}}}}}}
]

Fn([String, Int, Int], String). The len scalars starting at index start (zero-based), clipped to the String:
{"Hello!".substr 0 3} is "Hel".
$[# {substr s start len} {concat {take {drop {chars s} start} len}}]

Fn([String, String], List(String)). The pieces between occurrences of sep, a one-scalar String:
{"a,b,,c".split ","} is ["a", "b", "", "c"].
$[# {split s sep}
    [# {step acc c} {!if {= c sep} {. done {cons {.cur acc} {.done acc}} cur ""} {.cur= acc {++ {.cur acc} c}}}]
    [# r {fold {chars s} {. done {'} cur ""} step}]
    {reverse {cons {.cur r} {.done r}}}
]

Private. The format renderer over a template's scalars.
$[# {_render cs args}
    {K::caseList cs
        {^ {} {K::caseList args {^ {} ""} {^ {a r} {error "format: too many arguments"}}}}
        {^ {c rest}
            {!if {= c "{"}
                {K::caseList rest {^ {} {error "format: unmatched {"}} {^ {n more}
                    {!if {= n "{"} {++ "{" {_render more args}}
                        {!if {= n "}"}
                            {K::caseList args {^ {} {error "format: too few arguments"}} {^ {a r} {++ a {_render more r}}}}
                            {error "format: invalid slot"}}}}}
                {!if {= c "}"}
                    {K::caseList rest {^ {} {error "format: unmatched }"}} {^ {n more}
                        {!if {= n "}"} {++ "}" {_render more args}} {error "format: unmatched }"}}}}
                    {++ c {_render rest args}}}}}}
]

Fn([String, List(String)], String). Replaces each {} in the template by the next argument; {{ and }} are
literal braces. Too few or too many arguments is an error. For typed holes, prefer a template f"{...}".
$[# {format template args} {_render {chars template} args}]

The Type of formulas: a record holding LaTeX source, so hosts can render it as math.
$[# Latex [% {Struct {' {Field "latex" String}}}]]

Fn([String], Latex). A formula from its LaTeX source, often a template: {latex f"{\frac{1}{2}}"}.
$[# {latex source} {: {. latex source} Latex}]

Fn([Latex], String). The LaTeX source of a formula.
$[# latexSource .latex]

Private. One scalar of text, as LaTeX source.
$[# {_latexChar c}
    {!if {contains {' "{" "}" "$" "&" "#" "%" "_"} c} {++ "\\" c}
        {!if {= c "\\"} "\\text{\\textbackslash}"
            {!if {= c "^"} "\\text{\\textasciicircum}"
                {!if {= c "~"} "\\text{\\textasciitilde}" c}}}}
]

Fn([String], String). Text as LaTeX source that typesets it literally, for putting a name or a label into a
formula: {latexEscape "a_b"} is "a\_b".
$[# {latexEscape s} {concat {map {chars s} _latexChar}}]

9. Assertions, hashing, and host time

Eq A: Fn([A, A], A). Returns a if the values are equal, otherwise fails with both values.
$[# {assertEqual a b}
    {!if {= a b} a {error {concat {' "assertion failed: " {debug a} " is not equal to " {debug b}}}}}
]

{!assert a b}: checks during preparation that a equals b and returns a. Both must be statically available.
$[Macro !assert
    {{a b} [% {assertEqual a b}]}]

Eq A: Fn([A], Int). A stable hash: equal values hash equally on every platform and in every run.
$[# hash K::hash]

Fn([], Int), Runtime. The Unix time (seconds) that the host supplies for this evaluation; constant within it.
$[Native time {Fn {'} Int} "std.time/now" Runtime]
